test_scenarios.description:
Primary repro (idx=9, exact). File: `namespace eval ::app {}
proc ::app::Helper {} { return 42 }
set s [interp create -safe]
interp alias $s greet {} ::app::Helper
interp eval $s { greet }
`. Before fix: W123 'Unknown command greet' + 0 definition locations. After fix: 0 diagnostics; go-to-definition on `greet` resolves to the `::app::Helper` proc line. tclsh9.0-verified this prints 42 at runtime (scratchpad/audit/b).

Real-world idiom variant — the object-command form doctools.tcl actually uses, exercising handle_interp_handle_eval_command not handle_interp_eval_command: `namespace eval ::app {}
proc ::app::Helper {} { return 42 }
set mpip [interp create -safe]
interp alias $mpip greet {} ::app::Helper
$mpip eval { greet }
`. Expect: 0 diagnostics, go-to-definition on `greet` (inside `$mpip eval {...}`) resolves to ::app::Helper. This must NOT pass merely by fixing handle_interp_alias/handle_interp_eval_command — it specifically requires the handle_interp_handle_eval_command change.

Cross-contamination guard mirroring 26e5553's own namespace-eval test (handlers.rs:4624, two_dynamic_namespace_eval_blocks_sharing_a_variable_name_never_collide) and directly reproducing secondary issue #2: `proc makeA {} {
    set s [interp create -safe]
    interp eval $s {
        proc helper {} { return A }
        helper
    }
}
proc makeB {} {
    set s [interp create -safe]
    interp eval $s {
        proc helper {} { return B }
        helper
    }
}
`. BEFORE this fix (verified live): go-to-definition on makeA's `helper` call resolves into makeB's proc, and `references` on makeA's helper definition lists 3 locations spanning both procs. AFTER the fix (using per-call-site @autoname@<offset> keys, not raw '$s' text): each proc's `helper` call must resolve only to its own proc's definition; references on makeA's helper must list exactly 2 locations, both inside makeA.

A `$VAR` never bound by `set VAR [interp create ...]` must stay exactly as conservative as today — no crash, no false resolution. `proc useEval {ip} {
    interp eval $ip { someCmd }
}
`: `someCmd` is genuinely unresolvable (ip is a proc parameter, not a tracked binding) — expect the same behaviour as before the fix (whatever W123/no-op happens today for an unresolvable dynamic interp path), specifically NOT a crash and NOT a spurious W140 (dynamic path still abstains the existence check).

Builtins-only inside a dynamic interp eval body must remain silent, confirming the fix doesn't newly over-fire: `set s [interp create -safe]
interp eval $s {
    set x 1
    expr {$x + 1}
    puts "hi"
}
` (scratchpad/audit/c) — 0 diagnostics both before and after.

Minimal repro (verbatim, the confirmed finding's own e.tcl): `namespace eval ::e {
    namespace ensemble create -map {
        foo ::e::Foo
    }
}
proc ::e::Foo {args} { return "foo: $args" }

puts [e foo bar]
`. definition/hover at the "foo" call-site token (0-based line 7, col 8) must resolve to ::e::Foo's decl (line 5). Currently returns 0 locations / "(no hover)" — reverified live against a freshly-built server.

Differing-case subcommand + forward-declared target (mirrors widget.tcl's `make`): `namespace eval ::widget {
    namespace ensemble create -map {
        make ::widget::Make
    }
}
puts [widget make hello]
proc ::widget::Make {args} { return "make: $args" }
`. definition on "make" must resolve to ::widget::Make's decl even though (a) the target is declared LATER in the file and (b) the subcommand's spelling differs from the target's simple name in case — a fix that matched on inv.name == def_name (string equality) rather than resolved_qualified_name would fail this even after the analyser-side change.

The -subcommands sibling form (not -map), the folded-in secondary fix: `namespace eval ::box {
    proc show {} { return "shown" }
    namespace ensemble create -subcommands {show}
}
box show
`. definition on the second "show" (call site) must resolve to ::box::show's decl — proves record_ensemble_subcommands's half of the fix, independent of -map.

Must NOT regress the audit's own explicitly-correct abstention: dynamic map mutation via `namespace ensemble configure ... -map $var` (mirrors widget.tcl's addType/mapping case). `namespace eval ::widget { namespace ensemble create -map { make ::widget::Make } }
proc ::widget::addType {t} {
    set m [namespace ensemble configure ::widget -map]
    dict set m dyn ::widget::Dyn
    namespace ensemble configure ::widget -map $m
}
proc ::widget::Dyn {args} { return dyn }
widget addType foo
puts [widget dyn hello]
`. definition on "dyn" (call site) must still return 0 locations — the `dyn -> ::widget::Dyn` mapping only exists via a runtime dict-set + `-map $m` (variable) mutation, never a literal `-map {...}` list, so ensemble_subcommand_targets["::widget"] only ever contains {make: ::widget::Make}. A naive fix that tried to simulate the mutation would wrongly resolve this and contradict the audit's own stated correctness rule.

Two distinct ensembles in different namespaces sharing a subcommand name must not cross-contaminate (validates the outer HashMap is keyed per-ensemble, not a single flat table — the exact reason command_aliases can't be reused directly): `namespace eval ::a { namespace ensemble create -map {go ::a::Go} }
namespace eval ::b { namespace ensemble create -map {go ::b::Go} }
proc ::a::Go {} { return a }
proc ::b::Go {} { return b }
puts [a go]
puts [b go]
`. definition on "go" after "a" must resolve to ::a::Go; definition on "go" after "b" must resolve to ::b::Go.

Subcommand not present in the (fully static) map — a typo or a genuinely-unmapped name — must silently abstain (0 locations), not crash and not guess: `namespace eval ::widget { namespace ensemble create -map { make ::widget::Make } }
proc ::widget::Make {args} {}
puts [widget mak hello]
`. definition on "mak" returns 0 locations. (Also documents that Tier 1 deliberately does not implement Tcl's unique-prefix ensemble-subcommand matching — a real tclsh would actually dispatch "mak" to "make" here since it's an unambiguous prefix and -prefixes defaults to true; flagged as an explicit, reusable-algorithm-named follow-up, not attempted now, via ScopedCommand::subcommand / CommandSpec::resolve_subcommand_for_dialect's existing exact-or-unique-prefix logic.)

Whole -map value itself is dynamic (not just one entry) — must abstain exactly as today, zero regression: `namespace eval ::widget {
    variable dynamicMap {make ::widget::Make}
    namespace ensemble create -map $dynamicMap
}
proc ::widget::Make {args} {}
puts [widget make hello]
`. definition on "make" returns 0 locations (ensemble_subcommand_targets never gets an entry for "::widget" at all, since record_ensemble_map_targets's caller only invokes it when the -map's own value token is present as list text — a bare `$dynamicMap` token has no parseable pairs).

CORE FIX TARGET — full idiom (mirrors tcllib hook::Set / the original repro.tcl): `proc ::hooklib::Set {ns handler} {
    namespace eval $ns [list namespace unknown $handler]
}
namespace eval ::target {
    proc fallbackHandler {args} { return "handled:$args" }
    ::hooklib::Set ::target [namespace code fallbackHandler]
    proc run {} { return [mystery arg1 arg2] }
}
`. Ground truth (tclsh9.0/8.6): resolves via the installed handler chain, exits 0. Before fix: W123 HINT fires on `mystery` (false positive). After fix: 0 W123 diagnostics.

REGRESSION GUARD (negative control, no hook at all) — mirrors control_no_hook.tcl: `namespace eval ::target {
    proc run {} { return [mystery arg1 arg2] }
}
`. Ground truth: genuinely errors at runtime (`invalid command name "mystery"`). Must report 1 W123 diagnostic both before and after the fix — proves the fix isn't blanket-suppressing the token 'mystery' or namespace ::target.

proposed_changes.description:
Insert right after Phase 1's `for cmd in &cmds { ... }` loop closes (method_bodies is fully populated; class_def is still `&mut` in scope) and before Phase 2 begins: `self.collect_oo_links(&method_bodies, class_def);` where:

fn collect_oo_links(&self, method_bodies: &[CollectedMethodBody], class_def: &mut ClassDef) {
    for mb in method_bodies {
        if mb.body_tok.kind != TokenType::Str { continue; }
        let base = mb.body_tok.span.start() + u32::from(mb.body_tok.content_offset);
        let cmds = crate::segmenter::segment_commands_with_offset_and_config(&mb.body_text, base, self.lexer_config());
        for cmd in &cmds {
            if cmd.is_partial || cmd.texts.first().map(String::as_str) != Some("link") { continue; }
            for word in cmd.texts.iter().skip(1) {
                if crate::naming::is_dynamic_word(word) { continue; }
                match crate::tcl_expr_eval::split_tcl_list(word).as_slice() {
                    [name] if !name.is_empty() => { class_def.linked_members.entry(name.trim_start_matches("::").to_string()).or_insert_with(|| name.trim_start_matches("::").to_string()); }
                    [name, target] if !name.is_empty() => { class_def.linked_members.entry(name.trim_start_matches("::").to_string()).or_insert_with(|| target.clone()); }
                    _ => {}
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Deliberately top-level-only (no descent into if/catch/foreach body args inside the method body) — matches references.rs's scan_my_method_region's own existing, accepted scope boundary for the same class of problem. A dynamic name/target ($var, [cmd]) is skipped, matching detect_interp_alias's literal-only requirement. `entry().or_insert()` means the first `link` recorded for a given alias wins if it's declared more than once (deterministic, order-of-appearance in the class body).

Replace the unconditional `class_def.methods.get(word)` / `class_def.class_methods.get(word)` / `class_def.properties.get(word)` reads with:

if let Some(target) = class_def.linked_members.get(word) {
    if let Some(m) = class_def.methods.get(target) { return Some(m.name_span); }
    if let Some(m) = class_def.class_methods.get(target) { return Some(m.name_span); }
    if let Some(p) = class_def.properties.get(target) { return Some(p.name_span); }
}

...leaving the `word == "constructor"` / `word == "destructor"` arms immediately below completely UNCHANGED (see secondary issues — they serve a second, legitimate purpose this fix does not touch).

REQUIRED, easy to miss: both tests currently assert that a bare, un-linked sibling method/classmethod bareword call resolves (`src = "oo::class create C {
    method greet {} {}
    method twice {} { greet ; greet }
}
"`, asserts locs.len()==1) — this is EXACTLY the confirmed bug, pinned as "working". They WILL fail the moment the fix lands unless updated. Recommend: change each to assert `locs.is_empty()` (rename e.g. to `definition_bare_sibling_call_without_link_abstains` / `definition_bare_classmethod_call_without_link_abstains`, matching real tclsh's "invalid command name" for that exact source), and ADD a new sibling test per case with a `link greet` / equivalent added to a constructor, asserting resolution now happens for the correct reason. `definition_jumps_to_constructor_keyword` (~1938) and `definition_member_lookup_skipped_outside_class_body` (~1956) are unaffected — leave as-is.

test_scenarios.description:
Single-name link, primary repro fixed for the right reason: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { link foo }
  method foo {x} { return "foo-called:$x" }
  method bar {} { return [foo 42] }
}`. Hover/definition on bare `foo` inside `bar` must resolve to `method foo`'s declaration. Real tclsh: succeeds, `foo-called:42` (verified live).

Two-element alias form — closes a currently-live false negative: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { link {shortcut realMethod} }
  method realMethod {x} { return "real-called:$x" }
  method bar {} { return [shortcut 42] }
}`. Hover/definition on `shortcut` inside `bar` must resolve to `realMethod`'s declaration (not to a nonexistent member literally named `shortcut`). Real tclsh: succeeds, `real-called:42` (verified live). Current LSP: returns no hover/definition at all for `shortcut` (verified live) — this scenario is currently a silent gap, not just a false positive.

link called from destructor (not just constructor) is still detected: `oo::class create Widget {
  method foo {x} {return $x}
  method bar {} {return [foo 1]}
  destructor { link foo }
}`. Hover on `foo` inside `bar` resolves — `collect_method_body` covers constructor/method/destructor uniformly, and the scan must too.

PRIMARY CONFIRMED REPRO (idx=113) — must flip from resolving to abstaining: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { }
  method foo {x} { return "foo-called:$x" }
  method bar {} { return [foo 42] }
}`. Hover/definition on bare `foo` inside `bar` must become empty/None post-fix. Currently: hover="**method** `::Widget::foo` (1 param(s))", definition jumps to `foo`'s declaration (both confirmed live against current code). Real tclsh9.0/8.6: `invalid command name "foo"` (confirmed live).

classmethod variant: `oo::class create C {
  classmethod factory {} { return "made" }
  method use {} { return [factory] }
}`; driven via `C create c1; c1 use`. Hover/definition on `factory` inside `use` must become empty post-fix. Currently wrongly resolves (matches the pre-existing pinned test `definition_jumps_to_classmethod` / `class_member_hover_fires_for_classmethod`, which must be updated alongside this fix). Real tclsh9.0: `invalid command name "factory"` (confirmed live).

property variant (Tcl 9.0+, `oo::configurable`): `oo::configurable create Widget {
  property length -get {return 42}
  method use {} { return [length] }
}`; driven via `Widget create w1; w1 use`. Hover/definition on `length` inside `use` must become empty post-fix. Currently wrongly resolves to "**property** `::Widget::length`" (confirmed live). Real tclsh9.0: `invalid command name "length"` (confirmed live).

Precision guard — a link for a DIFFERENT name must not blanket-legitimize every bareword in the class: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { link foo }
  method foo {x} {return $x}
  method other {y} {return $y}
  method bar {} { return [other 1] }
}`. `other` was never linked (only `foo` was) — bare `other` inside `bar` must stay unresolved even though `link foo` appears elsewhere in the same class. Guards against an implementation that treats "class has any link call" as sufficient instead of matching the precise per-name map.

Regression guard — already-correct, must stay correct: control.tcl shape, bareword matching NO member at all: `oo::class create Widget {
  method foo {x} { return "foo-called:$x" }
  method bar {} { return [totallyUnknownBareword 42] }
}`. Hover/definition/diagnostics on `totallyUnknownBareword` stay empty before and after (untouched code path; diagnostics correctly emits nothing, matching TclOO method-body W123 abstention, confirmed live both before this fix and expected unchanged after).

`my foo` dispatch (the correct, always-valid idiom) must be completely unaffected: `oo::class create Widget {
  method foo {x} { return "foo-called:$x" }
  method bar_ok {} { return [my foo 99] }
}`. Hover/definition on `foo` after `my` resolves via `instance_method_definition`, a different code path entirely from `lookup_class_member` — sanity-check it is not collaterally affected.

Bareword matching a method name OUTSIDE any class body: `oo::class create Widget {
  method greet {} {}
}
greet`. Matches the existing pinned tests `definition_member_lookup_skipped_outside_class_body` / `class_member_hover_skipped_outside_class_body` — must stay empty (cursor never inside body_span), unaffected by this fix either way.

Dynamic link target must not crash and must not falsely resolve: `oo::class create Widget {
  variable which
  constructor {w} { set which $w; link $which }
  method foo {x} {return $x}
  method bar {} { return [foo 1] }
}`. `link $which` is skipped by the `is_dynamic_word` guard (mirrors detect_interp_alias's own literal-only requirement) — `foo` stays unresolved (conservative fallback-to-runtime, matches AGENTS.md's documented philosophy for exactly this kind of construct).

Fix must not be over-conservative — accumulation across multiple link-bearing bodies: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { link foo }
  method setup {} { link bar }
  method foo {x} {return $x}
  method bar {y} {return $y}
  method use {} { return "[foo 1] [bar 2]" }
}`. Hover on both `foo` and `bar` inside `use` must resolve — links recorded from different member bodies (not just the constructor) must all accumulate onto the same ClassDef.

Multiple single-name aliases in one `link` call: `oo::class create Widget {
  constructor {} { link foo bar }
  method foo {x} {return $x}
  method bar {y} {return $y}
  method use {} { return "[foo 1] [bar 2]" }
}`. `link foo bar` is TWO independent one-element arguments (not a single two-element pair) — both `foo` and `bar` must resolve, verifying the per-argument (not per-command) parsing of link's variadic argument list.
